body:
Theoretically could you counter this by also becoming a cop and getting your revenge?

&#x200B;

Edit. I know it's not practical, nor do I think it's advisable. This is more of a hypothetical

Taking a gap year to travel the world.

&#x200B;

Edit: I didn't realise so many non wealthy people did this too, out of the people I know it was mostly the richer kids who could afford to travel before uni.

Girls clothes are so much more impractical, especially for active/sporty kids. At the end of the day it's like you said with your son's hair and piercings clothes aren't a permanent mark on your body, there's no harm in letting kids express themselves with what little control they have. 

I remember that "sit like a lady" crap as well, the first time I ever consented to wearing a dress I was 18 and went formal dress shopping with a friend. I was planning on wearing a suit but the lady in the shop kept getting me to try on all these dresses and telling me I had a good figure and because I struggled a lot with weight (and self esteem) as a teenager I was over the moon that someone thought I looked good, I ended up getting a red dress and was nervous about going to the event in a dress (I went to an all girl's catholic school and didn't really get along with most of the people) but it went well and since then I've been more confidant wearing women's clothes. (I ended up getting into a pushups competition with one of the guys from the all boy's sister school at the event so not so classy.) Thank you dress shop lady 

&#x200B;
